f values and buzz concepts  that often bring  these people together including white  culturalism  again whatever that means  anti-multiculturalism  nativism and anti-immigration sentiment  anti-foreign interventionism  anti-semitism  men's rights activism and gamergate  which among  the alt-right is often seen as a very  serious  and important movement although the  phenomenon of the alt-right is so  loosely defined  and has taken many shapes over time most  people say that the movement originally  began developing during the bush  administration  as a reaction to traditional  conservatism neo-conservatism as well as  the republican party  a lot of alt-righters say that their  philosophy is derived from  paleo conservatism giving credit to  people like pat buchanan  sam francis and joe sobrin but the  alt-right as a vague  mass collection of online posters  definitely has its roots in the  sensationalist excitement around ron  paul's 2008 and 2012 runs for president  which eventually devolved to become more  socially conservative and  bigoted and the alt-right definitely  began to pick up momentum during the  obama administration in reaction to a  black and left-of-center  president the creation of the alt-right  is usually attributed to a blogger and  podcaster  named richard spencer spencer is a white  supremacist  who founded the website called  alternative right in 2010  where the alt-right got its name  milo yiannopoulos the current beloved  poster boy of the alt-right who is in  essence single-handedly responsible for  bringing the alt-right to mainstream  awareness via breitbart and other media  appearances  has plenty of times said that he does  not even consider himself to be part of  the alt-right  this as well as the fact that he's a  half-jewish and gay man  acting as the prophet of an anti-semitic  and homophobic  supposed ideology speaks to both the  gimmicky performance art style of milo's  persona  as well as the lack of coherence that  the alt-right has as a movement  people who identify as alt-right are  almost universally biased against both  muslims  and jews but like i said alt-writers are  not necessarily christians across the  board  many are actually atheists but a  prevalent theme among them is their  fondness for the idea of what they like  to think of as  christian values which is another one of  these concepts that's hard to pin down  as a value system but it can probably be  best described  as an adherence to social conservatism  but interestingly at the same time  despite their love for right-wing  christianity's influence on the west  most within the alt-right embrace the  notion of evolution and many of them  adulterate the theory of evolution to  the point of using it to justify racism  claiming that there are human races or  sub-species  that sometimes are smarter more moral or  better than others  and of course they see white non-jews as  being the best  and they use non-scientific quackery to  come to these conclusions again  obviously there's nothing new or  interesting about these ideas there's  just a new loud group on the internet  called the alt-right that's touting this  outdated nonsense  the one and only redeeming quality of  the alt-right  is their commitment against political  correctness and identity politics to the  extent that it's destructive  i'm actively looking for a silver lining  here and this is at least  something we've talked on the show about  this plenty of times about how  authoritarian leftism  would some refer to as the regressive  left can be totally counterproductive in  how it stifles conversation about a  number of things  whether it be race islam feminism  but this is what i truly find to be most  ironic and hypocritical about the  alt-right  how they try to use political  correctness and identity politics  with respect to white people and men  people in the alt-right go around saying  that women  and ethnic minorities and religious  minorities rely  too much 